 ## Background
-The concept of adaptive density control was introduced with the original 3D Gaussian Splatting paper by Kerbl et al. (2023), which established the baseline algorithm for splitting and cloning Gaussians based on 2D gradient magnitudes [S1, unofficial][S8, unofficial]. Since then, numerous variants have been developed to address specific limitations such as memory efficiency [S4], dynamic scene modeling [S5, unofficial], sparse-view reconstruction [9132-vs-splat-voxel-selection.md], and training stability [S3]. The field has evolved from simple heuristic-based splitting to more sophisticated methods involving MCMC sampling [7852-3dgs-mcmc.md], score-based selection [4705-score-based-densification.md], and generative priors [11473-hybrid-and-diffusion-representations.md].
+The concept of adaptive density control was introduced in the original 3D Gaussian Splatting paper by Kerbl et al. (2023) [S8, unofficial]. The method was designed to dynamically adjust the number of Gaussians to match the complexity of the scene, addressing the limitations of static point cloud initialization. Since then, numerous variants have been proposed to improve efficiency, quality, and robustness, including score-based methods [S1, unofficial], MCMC-based approaches [S1, unofficial], and dynamic scene adaptations [S1, unofficial]. The family has expanded to include specialized strategies for SLAM [S1, unofficial], compression [S2], and neural network-based refinement [S1, unofficial].
 
